jansson v2.15.1: fast paths for ASCII in the string dump, the UTF-8 check
and the lexer of a string token

PROPOSAL of the performance audit (performance/audit/), NOT applied: to
apply it, copy it to kernel/c/linux-ext-libs/patches/jansson/ and bump
VERSION in configure-libs.sh (every node that builds from source then
reruns ./extrae.sh && ./configure-libs.sh).

On top of 0001-load-stop-the-lexer-when-a-save-fails.patch.

What a record of timeranger2 costs is mostly jansson's: dumping it
(json_dumps: dump_string, utf8_iterate, utf8_check_*) was ~50% of an
append, and parsing it (json_loadb: stream_get, lex_scan, strbuffer_*) 82%
of a read. All three walk a string a byte at a time through a function
call, a UTF-8 decode and, in the lexer, a callback per byte and a save per
byte. For ASCII, which is most of what Yuneta stores and sends, none of
that is needed.

1. utf8_check_string(): a byte below 0x80 is skipped before
   utf8_check_first(), which answers 1 for it. Same answer.
2. dump_string(): a byte below 0x80 is its own codepoint, the next one
   after it: what utf8_iterate() answers. Same bytes written.
3. lex_scan_string(): reading from the buffer of json_loadb(), the run of
   plain ASCII that follows in a string token (0x20..0x7F but '"' and
   '\') is saved at once (lex_save_ascii_run()): the same bytes saved, the
   same position and column (no newline in such a run), and the saved text
   grown to the same size by the same doublings, so a token that fails for
   memory fails where it failed. Nothing is done when the source is
   another one, when the stream holds the rest of a multi-byte character,
   or when the growth fails: the bytes are then read one by one, as
   before.

Checked: jansson's own test suite (215 tests, among them the invalid
inputs with the line, column and position of their errors) passes; and
original and patched libraries, linked to the same driver, gave byte for
byte the same output on random documents (valid, mutated, invalid UTF-8,
escapes, control characters, strings of 3000 bytes; loaded with three sets
of flags, dumped with five, and json_stringn() of raw bytes): 15 runs of
4000 documents, and 15 more with the allocator capped at 300..3000 bytes,
thousands of "not enough memory" among them, with the same positions.

